# Vote on the Police Grant
Tamil title: காவல்துறை நிதிக் கோரிக்கை வாக்கெடுப்பு

Date: 5 July 1967

**Honourable C. N. Annadurai:** Mr Deputy Speaker, many members of this House have offered valuable views on the police grant. Party perspectives intervened from time to time, but I am very pleased that three important points have emerged from the debate.

First, everyone agrees that an efficient police department is essential to the social life of the country. Second, everyone agrees that an efficient force must be provided adequate facilities. Third, members have said that no outsider should interfere and bend the department to serve partisan interests.

No one can disagree with these three principles. Even people who have used the police for political ends would not openly argue that it ought to be so used; they know it is wrong. I therefore warmly welcome the unanimity expressed by this House.

I wish to add two considerations about making the police efficient. Whether it is the police, revenue, or any other department, it reflects the character of society; no department can stand outside society. We send our own brothers, relatives and neighbours into every department, including the police. The quality found in society will therefore be reflected in them. As society becomes more upright, some of the police department's shortcomings will diminish of themselves.

From the days of British domination, people entering the police were trained to exercise authority, uphold the rulers' power and protect the established order. That condition is certainly changing gradually. If those taking responsibility as police officers are to become still better qualified, their salaries, facilities and concessions must be improved enough to make everyone wish to join the service. [Part of the paragraph in Image 2 is badly damaged in OCR; this translation follows only its recoverable argument.]

The distance that once existed between the police and the public is also shrinking day by day. There are two chief reasons: police personnel have begun to recognise their responsibilities more fully, and the public has accepted that affairs cannot proceed without the police. As this understanding grows, I firmly believe that the department's scattered defects will gradually decline.

The Leader of the Opposition, Mr Karuthiruman, repeatedly said, “You keep telling the story of the past twenty years; do not go on repeating it.” I understand his genuine discomfort. Even those who recount it feel awkward. Yet is it not true? When my friends on this side refer to how things were done, I ask them to say at the same time, “We must not conduct ourselves in that manner.” Past events are cited to illuminate claims about the present, not simply to level accusations.

Our friend, the member from Rajapalayam, made another point. The police and other authorities lived for twenty years under the impression that only one party could govern and that an alternative administration would never walk the land. Since they expected the same party always to return, they felt no compulsion to change their outlook. Only now have they acquired confidence that another party can govern. If different parties alternate in office every five or ten years, no party will imagine that it can draw the police or any administrative department to its side.

Indeed, Mr Karuthiruman may receive greater respect at any police station than a legislator of the ruling DMK. Officials see in him the shadow of twenty years of Congress government and may fear that Congress could return next time. When members on my side arrive, they may think, “So these people have come, but perhaps only for a little while, not for ever.” Even out of office, Congress members may possess more influence than DMK members now in office. I say this from an understanding of human psychology. Congress members may imagine that they have lost everything and that everything they lacked has come to us, but such a condition will certainly not arise.

Disturbances, confusion and attacks are occurring here and there. My respected friend Rajangam from the Kurinjipadi constituency referred to one. When discussing disturbances, members should not repeatedly describe them as having occurred on a caste basis, for doing so can itself sow caste hostility. I make this request with humility.

Unlike some countries and some parts of India, Tamil Nadu has no condition in which one caste belongs to one political party. Every party contains members of every community in substantial numbers. The growth of parties of this kind will itself reduce caste disturbances. When a clash occurs, members should not hastily label it a fight between one caste and another or repeat other people's misunderstandings. They should conduct themselves so as to leave as little room as possible for caste feeling. Our State does not have caste disturbances on the scale sometimes suggested.

Two incidents in South Arcot district were described as disturbances. Neither began on a caste foundation. A quarrel may start with “the cow grazed there” and develop into “Do I not know you and your caste?” Only after politicians arrive and choose opposing sides does it acquire the name of a caste disturbance. Most clashes arise from an immediate dispute between two groups. If members try to use them to place the DMK in difficulty, all of us together will have manufactured caste trouble. We should reduce such talk as far as possible. I do not ask them to abandon it at once; habits acquired over time cannot be discarded overnight, but they can be reduced.

Some disturbances are also arising because Congress is no longer the ruling party. Amma Pettai in Salem district was cited. Are its incidents really between Congress on one side and the DMK on the other? On one side may stand an elder brother and on the other his younger brother; an uncle and a son-in-law; a shop owner and his partner. Those involved know this well. Amma Pettai is famous for thread and, by the same token, for tangled thread. The tangles there will resolve themselves. Do not bring every such matter to the Assembly and call it a Congress-DMK battle. Those who provoke the clash prove clever, while we politicians are deceived.

If someone tells Congress leaders, “A DMK man beat me only because I am a Congressman,” they should not accept it without inquiry. Likewise, if someone tells me, “Congressmen attacked me only because I belong to the DMK,” I am not prepared simply to believe him. Antisocial forces incite us against one another, like spectators at a cockfight who end by attacking each other.

You may cite letters and complaints alleging injury to Congress members. Mr Karuthiruman has the means to visit the place of any disturbance, learn the whole truth, and report it. If he does so, I promise to secure a just remedy within twenty-four hours. He showed me letters pinned together; many such letters are piled up at my home too. Rather than speak from letters alone, let us visit, investigate, and tell each other only what we finally find to be true. I am ready to seek the appropriate remedy.

A serious incident at Theni was mentioned. It was said that people were brought in handcuffs. I did not panic on hearing this; our movement lived with such treatment for many years. Why does it now cause alarm? After twenty years in authority, naturally they are shocked when handcuffs are mentioned.

**P. G. Karuthiruman:** We too have been handcuffed and dragged through the streets. We are not strangers to that experience, nor did I speak merely out of panic. I asked whether it was right to seize and handcuff a legislator who went to a police station requesting action.

**Annadurai:** Very well; I take it that the first part has been withdrawn and shall address the second. The name Theni immediately recalls old incidents that cannot be forgotten so easily. Minister Navalar Nedunchezhiyan is alive today because a stone thrown at him during a meeting there missed its mark. The assailant was identified as a Congressman and prosecuted, but the case was compromised to avoid prolonging bitterness. How can that memory disappear?

When we discuss the food crisis, do we not remember what British rule produced? Can the departed British say, “I left long ago; why still drag me into this?” If we once strike our head on a doorway, even nine years later we touch our head and say “Ah!” when crossing it. Remembering the sufferings we endured under your rule produces that same feeling. I do not say this merely to irritate you.

In some places the old habit continues: we were beaten at Theni then, and today it is said that Congress members receive the blows. If a DMK legislator has improperly intervened and ordered the police to do this or that, I am prepared to take party action against him. But the allegation that a legislator was handcuffed and dragged must be proved. Retired Justice Somasundaram and other retired judges are available. I am prepared to appoint any of them to conduct an impartial inquiry. [The final undertaking in Image 9 is severed by damaged OCR, but the following exchange establishes that Annadurai offered action if the allegation were proved.] What will the accusers do if the inquiry proves that it did not happen?

**Karuthiruman:** We shall drop it.

**Annadurai:** Ah, a fine answer: let us drop that allegation. In the Ramayana familiar to my friend, someone voiced suspicion about Sita and Rama ordered that she be taken blindfolded and left in the forest. “I say it happened; if you prove it did not, I shall drop it and say something else” must not become the character of a responsible opposition. It may be said in jest, but we should not yield to the itch to make one allegation or another merely on seeing us. First determine whether it is true. According to the information given to me, no one was brought there in handcuffs.

**Karuthiruman:** I accept an inquiry by a judge acting as a neutral person.

**Annadurai:** I am pleased that he accepts what I proposed. Yet, given our friendship and our long acquaintance in Parliament, legislative courtesy might have led him to tell me privately before raising it here. Had he done so, I might have awarded him a D or D-plus, as universities now grade marks. As matters stand, I give him only a B-plus! (Laughter.) I do not praise him too highly, though I am glad he agreed at least to this.

Members also speak of threats. Mr Jayaraj, I think, referred to them. Threatening letters do not come only to them; we too receive several every day. Here is one received yesterday by Minister Karunanidhi, not by me. Its wording shows that it was not written by a member of our movement. It threatens that Public Works Minister Karunanidhi will be shot wherever he may be in Tamil Nadu. The date in the OCR is unclear.

Mr Vinayagam will know that such letters usually arrive anonymously. Even if it carried a signature, I would not be prepared to attach importance to it. If such an end awaited us, none of us could desire a happier one. (Loud applause.) Some may similarly have threatened members opposite. Such letters reach us wherever we travel; it would not be proper to begin debating each of them in the Assembly.

For twenty years you were the favoured child. After only three months outside that position, you find it difficult to bear. I understand. A person whose great house has been auctioned and who must move elsewhere may look around and think, “Must I live here?” I read that the Leader of the Opposition said outside that he felt ashamed to sit in opposition. Why shame or pain? Is our aim not the growth of democracy?

It would sound nobler if he said, “We sacrificed office so that democracy might grow.” Future readers might then marvel at the loftiness of their purpose. They should instead reduce the display of their inner irritation through talk of shame and pain. If there is a real administrative wrong, I grant them every right to bring it forward immediately so it can be corrected.

We may govern today and another party may govern tomorrow. Whoever rules must not use influence to corrupt any department, least of all the police. Nor should we legislators belonging to political parties clash with one another. Suppose you approach a police officer and say that something he did was wrong. A cunning officer need only utter two suggestive words. At once one may infer, “The DMK government is in office; perhaps Minister Karunanidhi instructed him.” Officers spoke similarly to us in the past. Antisocial forces exploit politicians, and officials sometimes place responsibility for their own actions on political parties and escape. We become trapped because we fail to understand one another.

When we in the opposition suffered wrongs and approached police or other officials, they themselves sometimes said, “Our conscience knows this is unjust, but what can we do? You know whose government it is.” Today they may say the same thing to you. By learning about the officials, we may first learn to understand one another; then many apparent complications will be seen not to be complications at all.

We agree on three principles concerning this department: it must be made efficient; its personnel must receive the necessary facilities and salary increases; and politically minded people must not try to turn it to their own course. This cannot be put on a July programme and completed by September. It must be achieved gradually.

I said that society's attitudes are reflected in its departments. The respected member Mr Balasubramaniam said that the police have long stood on the side of property owners and, in disputes between employers and workers, on the employers' side. He said thousands of examples could be given. This happens because our social and political outlook, and the machinery of government, have been formed that way. The condition must change gradually.

Visitors to England say that a police officer there will immediately give clear directions when asked the way. Reading such a report, if someone asks a Mount Road traffic policeman for the way to Chintadripet, the officer may become irritated rather than answer. The London policeman reflects England's political and social environment. Ours reflects our own.

Even on a train we speak to one another only if we feel inclined. Ask someone how many children he has and he may ask whether he is obliged to tell you; ask his age and he may demand why you want to know. The same qualities appear in the police because police personnel are not separate commodities imported from Africa or America. They are blood of our blood and flesh of our flesh, our own siblings. As blood can develop impurities and flesh a swelling, defects arise here too.

The blood must therefore be purified: society must become cleaner. Political and economic attitudes must shift from the side of property owners to that of working people. This cannot be written overnight like a line in a play and brought into being at dawn. Mountains must be crossed little by little. I doubt whether we shall reach the destination within my lifetime, but taking the first step on that road will itself be satisfying.

Whatever party we belong to, we must care for the police department. Members have offered views about housing and other opportunities that should be provided to its personnel, and I welcome them. I can only welcome them, for because of the financial position I have been unable to provide even as much housing as I had hoped. I offer my grateful respects to members of every party for their varied suggestions about providing these facilities, and ask the House to pass the grant without a cut.

---NOTES---
The source identifies this as Speech 18, delivered in the Legislative Assembly on 5 July 1967 during voting on the police grant.

The Tamil OCR source is preserved verbatim in the bilingual output. Image 2 contains severe line fragmentation, and the end of Image 9 loses part of Annadurai's promised response to a judicial finding. These gaps are marked rather than reconstructed.

“வகுப்பு” is rendered contextually as caste or community in the discussion of local clashes, not as economic class.

The Amma Pettai “thread/tangled thread” passage is a place-based pun and is retained explicitly.

The threatening letter's date is damaged in OCR and is not normalised. Its violent wording is translated as part of the historical legislative record, not endorsed.

The source OCR reads “7008 examples” in the employer-worker passage; context strongly suggests a corrupted large-number expression, so the translation says “thousands” and the reading is logged for scan review.
